[COMMENTS]
STATION,Velocity area station. Flows modestly affected by hydro-electric power scheme. GENERAL: Velocity area station (approx. 20m wide) in a long straight reach. There is a flood bank on the lb and retaining wall on the rb. STATION HYDROMETRY: The low to medium flow control is an informal broad-crested weir. At higher flows when the weir drowns out, natural channel features will become more prevalent including some sharp bends in the river downstream. Old Howford Bridge 1.5k downstream may be a influential control feature at very high flows. All flows are contained in the channel. Peak flow ratings derived from current meter gaugings up to 1.5m and are based on simple extrapolation beyond this stage. FLOW RECORD: Missing data during May 2021 due to weir collapse. Full period of record peak flow data reviewed and released in August 2021 (WINFAP Files v10). FLOW REGIME: A responsive, natural catchment but the flow pattern is modestly affected (especially at low flows) by the operation of a small hydro-electric power scheme 1km upstream. Some open cast mining in upper areas.
CATCHMENT,A catchment of rugged topography draining W from Southern Uplands. Complex bedrock - Carboniferous sediments and igneous outcrops dominate; extensive superficial deposits (mostly Boulder Clay and peat). Hill grazing is the main land use; some forestry. No significant catchment changes known.
Qmed Suitability,Station has a well-defined channel profile and stable control.
Pooling Suitability,The extent of rating extrapolation above the highest gauging is deemed too large to have reasonable confidence in the flow estimate of the highest flood.
[END]
